    "C68","C70","C72","C73","C81"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "No supplied record directly measures the query diltiazem-type benzothiazepine absolute oral bioavailability; the visible L1 gold records for structurally or mechanistically relevant contexts are all above the 20% threshold (apixaban 49–50% human, 30% rat, 70–88% dog; tamoxifen ~100%; encainide 76–78%; midodrine 90–100%; pentopril 50–70%; cefroxadine ~100%).",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C13","C14","C16","C17","C18","C19","C20","C21","C22","C23","C24","C25","C26","C35","C42","C44"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Apixaban, sharing methoxyphenyl and lactam features with the query, remains above F=20% even under strong CYP3A4/P-gp induction (37–39% with rifampin), so metabolic or efflux liability alone does not establish F<20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C13","C19","C20","C36","C39"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The only sub-20% values in visible records are species- or metabolite-specific tamoxifen records (horse F 13.15%; 4-OHT poor due to glucuronidation) and do not transfer to the query's human oral context.",
      "card_ids": ["C28","C31"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4/L5 records for basic amines (imipramine 100% human absorption; diphenhydramine, chlorpromazine, and promethazine high Caco-2 permeability; compound 16a Papp A-B 19.9×10^-6 cm/s with ER 0.7) support efficient intestinal absorption for the query's basic amine, moderate logD profile; duplicate records are counted once.",
      "card_ids": ["C59","C60","C62","C63","C64","C67","C68","C69","C70","C72","C73","C74","C80","C81"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L6 hepatic-clearance records are for tamoxifen, imipramine, perazine, and 5-MeO-DMT, not diltiazem; they show CYP-mediated metabolism or microsomal clearance in other molecules but do not provide affirmative threshold-relevant low-F evidence for the query, and tamoxifen's ~100% F shows CYP metabolism can coexist with high bioavailability.",
      "card_ids": ["C82","C83","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91","C92","C93"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "hepatic_clearance_metabolic_stability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C82","C83","C84","C85","C86","C87","C88","C89","C90","C91","C92","C93"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct diltiazem-specific absolute F measurement is present in any supplied record.",
    "No diltiazem-specific hepatic clearance or metabolic stability assay is present; L6 records are for tamoxifen, imipramine, perazine, and 5-MeO-DMT.",
    "L3 AUC/Cmax values lack an IV comparator and cannot yield absolute bioavailability.",
    "First-pass ester hydrolysis and CYP3A4/P-gp contributions for the query are not directly quantified.",
    "Transfer from apixaban, tamoxifen, and basic amine records remains analog-based."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L6 hepatic-clearance/metabolic-stability records are not diltiazem-specific and do not provide affirmative threshold-relevant low-F evidence; they show CYP-mediated metabolism in other molecules but cannot overturn the high prediction supported by above-threshold gold records and favorable absorption/efflux evidence. Prediction remains high with moderate confidence."
}

